        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "a3893fb5-af83-54bc-9080-ce1c6dd2bd60",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Harry Emerson Fosdick, Dorothy Thompson, Kermit Roosevelt Jr.\n- Organizations: First Presbyterian Church, Riverside Church, American Friends of the Middle East\n- Places: New York City, Manhattan, West Village, Morningside Heights\n\nFacts:\n- Harry Emerson Fosdick was born on May 24, 1878, and he was an American pastor.\n- Harry Emerson Fosdick, although a Baptist, served as pastor at First Presbyterian Church in Manhattan's West Village in New York City.\n- Riverside Church is a Christian church in Morningside Heights, Upper Manhattan, New York City.\n- Riverside Church opened on October 5, 1930.\n- Harry Emerson Fosdick later served as pastor at the historic inter-denominational Riverside Church in Morningside Heights, Manhattan.\n- The American Friends of the Middle East was formed in 1951 by Dorothy Thompson, Kermit Roosevelt Jr., and 24 other American educators, theologians, and writers, including Harry Emerson Fosdick.\n- The American Friends of the Middle East was a pro-Arabist organization that was often critical of U.S. support for Israel.\n- Harry Emerson Fosdick died on October 5, 1969.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "4bd548a6-2890-5a08-a2a4-4f1912e6527b",
        "text": "Page: Harry Emerson Fosdick. Sentence: Harry Emerson Fosdick (May 24, 1878 – October 5, 1969) was an American pastor.\nPage: Harry Emerson Fosdick. Sentence: Although a Baptist, he was called to serve as pastor, in New York City, at First Presbyterian Church in Manhattan's West Village, and then at the historic, inter-denominational Riverside Church in Morningside Heights, Manhattan.\nPage: Riverside Church. Sentence: Riverside Church is a Christian church in Morningside Heights, Upper Manhattan, New York City.\nPage: Riverside Church. Sentence: It opened its doors on October 5, 1930.\nPage: American Friends of the Middle East. Sentence: The American Friends of the Middle East (AFME) was a pro-Arabist organization often critical of U.S. support for Israel that was formed in 1951 by columnist Dorothy Thompson, Kermit Roosevelt, Jr., and 24 further American educators, theologians, and writers (including Harry Emerson Fosdick).",
